WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:04.725
Rust makes failure explicit through
`Result` and absence explicit through `Option`,

2
00:00:04.805 --> 00:00:08.798
but code can still become fragile if
expected failures are handled with `unwrap`,

3
00:00:08.878 --> 00:00:10.614
`expect`, or ordinary panic paths.

4
00:00:10.694 --> 00:00:13.414
These tools are useful in examples,
prototypes, tests,

5
00:00:13.494 --> 00:00:17.198
and places where failure truly means the
program invariant has been broken.

6
00:00:17.278 --> 00:00:21.329
They become anti-patterns when predictable
production conditions are treated as

7
00:00:21.409 --> 00:00:22.046
impossible.

8
00:00:22.126 --> 00:00:25.771
Many failures are ordinary and should be
part of the design: missing files,

9
00:00:25.851 --> 00:00:28.861
invalid input, unavailable services,
permission problems,

10
00:00:28.941 --> 00:00:32.355
malformed configuration, unsupported
versions, expired sessions,

11
00:00:32.435 --> 00:00:35.155
failed network calls, and inconsistent
external data.

12
00:00:35.235 --> 00:00:37.377
In services, CLIs, libraries, and parsers,

13
00:00:37.457 --> 00:00:40.524
these cases need clear behavior rather
than abrupt surprises.

14
00:00:40.604 --> 00:00:42.571
`Result` and `Option` are design tools.

15
00:00:42.651 --> 00:00:46.297
They force the code to say what happens
when a value is absent or an operation

16
00:00:46.377 --> 00:00:47.246
cannot complete.

17
00:00:47.326 --> 00:00:50.971
The response may be rejection, retry,
fallback, controlled termination,

18
00:00:51.051 --> 00:00:53.771
fail-closed behavior, operator-facing
diagnostics,

19
00:00:53.851 --> 00:00:55.646
or a sanitized user-facing message.

20
00:00:55.726 --> 00:00:58.388
The important part is that the decision is
deliberate.

21
00:00:58.468 --> 00:01:01.303
Error handling is also a security and
operations concern.

22
00:01:01.383 --> 00:01:04.798
User-facing errors should not leak
secrets, internal paths, tokens,

23
00:01:04.878 --> 00:01:07.251
database details, or sensitive
configuration.

24
00:01:07.331 --> 00:01:11.497
Operator-facing logs need enough detail to
support investigation without exposing

25
00:01:11.577 --> 00:01:13.024
data that should be redacted.

26
00:01:13.104 --> 00:01:17.387
Libraries should avoid panicking for
caller-controllable conditions when returning

27
00:01:17.467 --> 00:01:19.087
an error would preserve control.

28
00:01:19.167 --> 00:01:23.160
A useful review practice is to search for
panic-oriented calls and classify them.

29
00:01:23.240 --> 00:01:25.845
Is the condition truly impossible after
validation?

30
00:01:25.925 --> 00:01:27.082
Is this test-only code?

31
00:01:27.162 --> 00:01:28.551
Is the failure recoverable?

32
00:01:28.631 --> 00:01:31.004
Would a caller expect an error instead of
a crash?

33
00:01:31.084 --> 00:01:33.688
Panics should mark exceptional invariant
failures,

34
00:01:33.768 --> 00:01:35.620
not replace ordinary error handling.
